I’ll first inspect the pattern, metadata, and the frozen PXRD workflow/tooling, then build a candidate inventory and refine it in GSAS-II. I’ll keep phase inclusion residual-led and verify the exported CIFs, fractions, profile, and project against one another before submission.

The chemistry points to a low-temperature Ba–W–O/C system, so BaWO₄, residual WO₃, and BaCO₃ are initial candidates—not conclusions. I’m now checking the available reference index and extracting peak families so candidate records can be screened by composition, symmetry, geometry, and source rather than by formula alone.

The first peak-family comparison is decisive for two phases: monoclinic WO₃ accounts for the shifted 23.43/23.91/24.65° triplet and related 33–35° lines, while orthorhombic witherite BaCO₃ accounts for the independent 24.21, 42.29, 45.17, and 47.08° families. A weak BaWO₄ contribution remains on the watch list because its strongest line overlaps the 26.9° region; I’ll test it only after establishing a common background/profile with the two secure phases.

I’ve selected physically credible source records for the first controlled comparison: ambient witherite COD 9010928 and several monoclinic WO₃ records spanning the plausible symmetry/setting alternatives. The next screen holds size, strain, texture, roughness, and transparency neutral while refining only a smooth background, both scales, one angular correction, cells, and a shared nonnegative U/W/X profile.

The first full two-phase refinement reaches Rwp ≈ 8.10% with ~55 wt% BaCO₃ and ~45 wt% WO₃, confirming both phases quantitatively. Its shared width terms run beyond the instrument’s physically allowed generation range, exactly the expected sign that phase-specific size/strain must replace an over-broad common profile; I’m using that as a baseline diagnostic, not accepting those nuisance values. Alternative WO₃ records are being compared under the same model.

The watch-list test rejects BaWO₄ under the same nuisance model: its scale collapses to numerical zero while its cell becomes unstable, and its nominally unique reflections are already explained by WO₃/BaCO₃ overlaps. I’m now rebuilding the fit with the redundant histogram scale fixed and testing size versus microstrain phase-by-phase; this should separate genuine specimen broadening from the inflated common instrument widths seen in the screening fit.

The controlled profile trials favor crystallite-size broadening for each phase over isotropic microstrain (Rwp improvements are larger for size in both independent tests). With both sizes active, the fit is 7.62% at about 53.7/46.3 wt% and sizes near 0.084/0.069 μm. I’m now releasing the common U/W/X terms around that physically adequate model, then I’ll test whether any residual strain, asymmetry, anisotropy, or texture is replay-stable.

The physical profile now passes the intended checks: releasing U or W produces a negative U or out-of-range W and unstable sizes, so those trials are rejected; a WO₃-only isotropic strain term is supported, while BaCO₃ strain is marginal. SH/L gives a small reproducible improvement at a plausible value (~0.037). The remaining structured residuals sit on modeled reflections rather than forming a new phase family, so I’m moving to staged heavy-atom and oxygen-coordinate refinement with geometry checks.

Heavy-atom refinement improves Rwp from 7.38% to 7.27% with only small, plausible shifts. Refining BaCO₃ light-atom positions gives 7.20% and improves the carbonate geometry (C–O 1.31–1.38 Å), so I’m retaining it. Unrestrained WO₃ oxygen refinement reaches 6.73% but creates O–O contacts near 1.99 Å and overlarge coordinate shifts, so that apparent gain is rejected; the neutron-derived WO₃ oxygen framework remains the defensible model.

A common BaCO₃ Uiso is supported only when WO₃ Uiso remains fixed; WO₃ otherwise refines negative and is rejected. Polarization refines to 0.793 and improves the cross-phase angular intensity trend. Holding that physical value and refining BaCO₃ Uiso gives the current Rwp 6.96% and ~54.4/45.6 wt%. I’m now comparing no texture against the symmetry-distinct [100], [010], [001], [110], and [111] March–Dollase axes, one phase at a time.

Texture is phase-specific: BaCO₃ shows a clear, axis-selective improvement for [110] (March–Dollase 0.919; Rwp 6.80%), whereas every WO₃ axis stays essentially random (0.994–1.015) with negligible gain. I’m retaining only BaCO₃ [110] texture. The last profile challenge is WO₃ uniaxial microstrain along each symmetry-distinct principal direction; unsupported anisotropy will be pruned.

WO₃ anisotropic strain is not supported: all three principal-axis models improve Rwp by at most 0.008% and stay close to isotropic. I’ve pruned them. The provisional model is now Rwp 6.80% at roughly 54.8 wt% BaCO₃ / 45.2 wt% WO₃. Before finalizing, I’m re-challenging BaWO₄ and the chemically plausible Ba–W oxide intermediates under this cleaned common profile.

The clean-profile re-challenge finds a statistically significant but very small BaWO₄ contribution (~0.43 wt%) across several partly independent families (26.8, 43.3, 53.9/54.8, and 69.3°). I’m keeping it on the inventory rather than discarding it. However, the sole exact COD record uses split half-occupied oxygen sites and produces implausibly short O–O contacts, so I’m applying the requested analogue workflow to replace that flawed record with a physically ordered scheelite parent before accepting the phase.

The exact BaWO₄ record contains split oxygen sites with unphysical short contacts, so I’m replacing that record—not the phase assignment—with an ordered scheelite analogue. I’m narrowing the analogue search to chemically closest parents, then I’ll re-fit its scale and cell against the clean common profile.

Three ordered parents are physically usable: BaMoO₄ keeps the Ba sublattice and requires Mo→W, while SrWO₄ and CaWO₄ keep W and require alkaline-earth substitution. I’m carrying all three oxygen frameworks through the same refinement so the weak-phase decision is based on diffraction agreement and geometry, not a single database choice.

All three ordered models recover the same weak phase at 0.46–0.47 wt% and essentially identical Rwp, so the phase signal is robust but its oxygen coordinates are not independently resolvable. The CaWO₄-derived geometry is the most balanced of these three; I’m checking the full indexed analogue pool once more for an even closer ordered tetrahedral geometry before freezing the model.

The 290 K neutron PbWO₄ parent gives an ordered Ba-substituted scheelite model with W–O 1.84 Å and no short O–O contacts; it fits at 0.468 wt%, indistinguishable from the other ordered prototypes. I’m adopting it provisionally and now re-challenging the other weak Ba–W–O candidates after rebuilding the common three-phase profile, as required when the inventory changes.

Ba₃W₂O₉ now meets the weak-phase criterion: its scale is 5.2σ above zero, its cell shifts only ~0.1%, and independent families near 26.31°, 40.88°, 43.05°, 53.77/54.68°, and 63.15° reduce the residual. I’m retaining it provisionally despite intensity mismatch at 30.91°, and I’m repeating the final watch-list challenge against this four-phase baseline.

The repaired Ba₀.₁₅WO₃ bronze collapses to zero scale, and Ba₃WO₆ only gains fit by distorting its low-symmetry cell by several percent; both are rejected. The provisional inventory is therefore BaCO₃, monoclinic WO₃, scheelite BaWO₄, and trace Ba₃W₂O₉. I’m now replacing the arbitrary 0.1 μm widths on the two trace phases with explicit neutral/size/strain comparisons before the final refinement.

The neutral-width trials are still refining; one candidate width has already wandered into an invalid Bragg-domain calculation, which is a useful sign that the trace-phase broadening is underdetermined. I’ll retain only terms that converge reproducibly with positive scales and physical widths.

The unstable trials are isolated to trace-phase size/strain parameters; the main-phase profile remains unchanged. I’m letting the comparison finish so the final project can document the simplest stable choice instead of inheriting a bound-hitting nuisance term.

The trace-phase widths are not identifiable: free sizes collapse below 0.01 μm or drive a scale to zero, while free strains explode to 77,000–154,000 ppm. I’m therefore pruning those terms and using the neutral sharp-profile model; this lowers the modeled traces to roughly 0.27 wt% BaWO₄ and 0.19 wt% Ba₃W₂O₉ and makes their fraction uncertainty explicit. Next I’m comparing zero offset with specimen displacement under that pruned model.

The geometry comparison is reconverging every phase cell and scale for each correction, so it is slower than a single-parameter test. This is intentional: it prevents the cells from making the zero/shift decision appear better or worse simply because they were frozen at the previous solution.

No new instability has appeared; the remaining runtime is the repeated full-profile reconvergence. After this comparison I’ll freeze the chosen geometry, perform a save/reopen/replay check, and generate the exact CIF/profile/result bundle from that project.

The specimen-displacement branch is the expensive one because the angle-dependent correction couples to all four cell metrics. It is still progressing; I’m keeping the comparison rather than defaulting to the earlier zero-offset model, since the final geometry needs to be defensible.

The displacement/no-correction branches did not produce stable covariance solutions, while the zero-offset branch reconverged normally. I’m keeping the single zero correction, fixing the already-established SH/L asymmetry value to remove its 98% covariance with zero, and pruning the unused Uiso constraint before the final save/reopen cycle.

The pruned model now reopens and reconverges on the full 10–100° range. Final modeled fractions are approximately 54.55% BaCO₃, 44.98% WO₃, 0.274% BaWO₄, and 0.192% Ba₃W₂O₉. I’m now running the evaluator-style atomic replay to ensure the saved project—not cached arrays—reproduces the profile with no physical review flags.

The fresh atomic replay passed: all four root CIFs are byte-identical to fresh exports from `final.gpx`, the original 8,301-point scan is preserved, and the replay reports no negative background, width, displacement, or polarization flags. All exported structures also pass contact checks, including ordered BaWO₄ with W–O 1.836 Å.

The final weak-phase evidence remains coherent after pruning broadening: BaWO₄ is 8.6σ above zero and lowers weighted χ² by 304; Ba₃W₂O₉ is 5.4σ and lowers it by 128 across several families, though its strongest 30.94° line is mismatched. I’m recording that distinction explicitly: the inventory is supported, but the trace-phase structures/fractions are less reliable than the main phases.

Validation found one formatting-only issue: `fit.csv` rounded the angle column to eight decimals, so its parsed grid was within tolerance but not bit-for-bit identical to the original. I’m increasing numeric precision and rerunning the final consistency checks; the project and CIFs themselves already match exactly.

Completed the GSAS-II refinement with four crystalline phases:

- BaCO₃ (witherite): 54.5500 wt%
- Monoclinic WO₃: 44.9840 wt%
- BaWO₄ (scheelite): 0.2743 wt%
- Ba₃W₂O₉: 0.1917 wt% (probable trace assignment)

Fresh full-grid replay gives Rwp = 6.7642% and Rp = 5.1820%, with no physical review flags. Trace-phase structures and fractions carry greater systematic uncertainty, as detailed in the report.

